      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "To determine whether hyperbaric oxygen (HBO) therapy could accelerate recovery from exercise-induced muscle damage in humans."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "DESIGN",
          "content": "Pretest-posttest design with random assignment to either a treatment (HBO) or placebo control (sham) group."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "SETTING",
          "content": "University of Alberta and Misericordia Hospital, Edmonton."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "PARTICIPANTS",
          "content": "12 healthy male students (24.2 +/- 3.2 years) who were unaccustomed to strenuous eccentric exercise of the calf muscles."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "INTERVENTIONS",
          "content": "All subjects performed a strenuous eccentric exercise protocol designed to elicit muscle damage within the right gastrocnemius muscle. Subjects subsequently received either HBO (100% oxygen at 253 kPa [2.5 ATA] for 60 min; n = 6) or sham (atmospheric air at 132 kPa [1.3 ATA] for 60 min; n = 6) treatment conditions. The first treatment was administered 3-4 hours after damage, with a second and third at 24 and 48 hours after the first, respectively."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "MAIN OUTCOME MEASURES",
          "content": "Dependent variables included peak torque at 0.52 radians/s, peak isometric torque, and muscular endurance using isokinetic dynamometry; muscle cross-sectional area using magnetic resonance imaging; inorganic phosphate levels and T2 relaxation time using 31P and 1H magnetic resonance spectroscopy; pain sensation and unpleasantness using the Descriptor Differential Scale. These variables were assessed at baseline and until day 5 postdamage."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "There was little evidence of a difference in recovery rate between the HBO and sham groups. Faster recovery was observed in the HBO group only for isometric peak torque and pain sensation and unpleasantness."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "HBO cannot be recommended as an effective method of treatment of this form of muscle injury."
        }
      ]},
